    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "status_as_recorded": "untestable",
      "finding": "Inferred defensive scheme changes (within and across games) exhibited near-uniform transition matrices and could not separate real adjustments from offense execution changes. The document caveat states that label error and classification noise made the question untestable."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-010",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Play-type PPP change across playoff series game buckets showed no clear signal (e.g., DRIVE PPP was stable or improved). Observational with selection/regression-to-the-mean caveats; no confidence intervals provided."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-044",
      "status_as_recorded": "inconclusive",
      "finding": "Opponent next-trip aftershock (H17) gave a real ~+0.12 PPP lift but led to artifact when used in net_swing. Own-sequence conditioning was ~0.01 PPP noise. A note mentioned 'Suppression control opponent same PT H1-H2 +0.011 PPP,' indicating that the H2-H1 scoring suppression effect on a single play type was very small."
    }
  ],
  "failed_approaches": [
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "what_failed": "Attempt to establish a reliable player-season trait from half-to-half Connector quality differences using split-half correlation.",
      "why": "Measurement reliability too low; most variance was within-player half-to-half session noise. Risk code: MEASURE_RELIABILITY."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "what_failed": "Using inferred defensive scheme change labels to detect adjustments within and between games.",
      "why": "Inferred labels could not separate real defensive changes from offensive execution variation; near-uniform transition matrices consistent with label noise. Risk code: LABEL_ERROR."
    },
    {
      "id": "FAM:SF-CHT-028",
      "what_failed": "Testing stability of action_sequences as a tactical signal.",
      "why": "Instability (CV=0.919) and low sample per pattern (avg 6.9); question was unanswerable due to drift/nonstationarity. Risk code: DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY."
    }
  ],
  "evidence_establishes": [
    "Player-level half-to-half scoring-metric differences (like Q_p) have low split-half reliability (r~0.215, ICC~0.216), suggesting that short-window scoring changes are dominated by within-subject noise.",
    "Inferred defensive strategy changes suffer from severe label error, making it difficult to link defensive intent to opponent scoring outcomes.",
    "For play-type specific suppression, the H1-to-H2 change in opponent PPP may be as small as 0.011, indicating that any underlying defensive effect on half-to-half scoring is likely very small.",
    "Tactical signals and half-to-half scoring differences in basketball are often unstable and require large sample sizes to overcome noise and regression to the mean."
  ],
  "evidence_does_not_establish": [
    "Whether a team-level metric of opponent H2-H1 scoring change, after proper adjustment for opponent quality, game script, and regression to the mean, could be a reliable trait.",
    "The required sample size (in team-seasons or possessions) to detect a reliable effect if the true ICC is, say, 0.2 or smaller.",
    "The causal isolation of a defense's contribution to opponent halftime adjustments, as opposed to opponent self-adjustment or fatigue effects."
  ],
  "own_inferences": [
    "Given the small suppression effect (~0.011 PPP) documented for similar half-to-half change, any reliable team-level signal would require a very large number of games per team-season to overcome random variance.",
    "With 120 team-seasons (each ~82 games), split-half reliability based on half-seasons (~41 games each) yields about 120 data points, which is likely underpowered for an ICC below 0.2–0.3; power may be inadequate unless the true reliability is substantially higher than the values seen for player-level metrics.",
    "Regression to the mean from extreme first-half performances will confound the H2-H1 difference unless explicitly modeled (e.g., using Bayesian adjustment or opponent-adjusted residuals), and residual confounding may still attenuate reliability."
  ],
  "uncertainties": [
    "The true magnitude of a defense's causal effect on opponent half-to-half scoring change, after controlling for opponent and situational factors, is unknown. It could be effectively zero.",
    "It is unclear whether 120 team-seasons will provide sufficient statistical power to detect reliability, even if a design with rigorous controls is used. A formal power analysis is needed but would itself depend on unknown parameters.",
