        "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
        "text": "Some team-seasons may face an unbalanced set of opponents (e.g., weak first-half schedules), reducing the effective sample size per half and the ability to adjust for opponent quality without overfitting."
      }
    ]
  },
  "prior_work": {
    "already_answered": "partly",
    "cited": [
      "FAM:SF-APPS-085",
      "FAM:SF-APPA-005",
      "FAM:SF-CHT-044"
    ],
    "summary": "Prior work found low split-half reliability for a player-level half-to-half metric (SF-APPS-085), label noise in inferred defensive adjustments (SF-APPA-005), and tiny suppression effects on opponent play-type H1-to-H2 PPP (+0.011, SF-CHT-044). These findings suggest that any team-level defensive effect on opponent H2-H1 scoring change will be difficult to measure reliably and may require much larger samples than currently available."
  },
  "risks": [
    {
      "code": "MEASURE_RELIABILITY",
      "text": "Opponent H2-H1 scoring differences per team are noisy, and the underlying signal may be too small to yield a reliable team trait."
    },
    {
      "code": "RTM_SELECTION",
      "text": "Regression to the mean from extreme first-half performances can dominate the H2-H1 difference, confounding the assessment of reliability."
    },
    {
      "code": "CONFOUNDING",
      "text": "Opponent offensive strength, schedule, and game context variables must be controlled; failure to do so will bias reliability estimates."
    },
    {
      "code": "EXPOSURE_SPARSITY",
      "text": "With only 41 games per half per team, some teams may face an uneven mix of opponent strengths, limiting the precision of adjusted estimates."
    },
    {
      "code": "DRIFT_NONSTATIONARITY",
      "text": "Roster changes, injuries, and strategic evolution over a season can change a team's defensive effectiveness, reducing between-half consistency."
    }
  ],
  "predicted_outcome": {
    "supported": 0.10,
    "rejected": 0.25,
    "untestable": 0.50,
    "artifact": 0.10,
    "uncertain": 0.05
  },
  "decision": "do_not_proceed",
  "design": {
    "estimand": "The split-half reliability (Spearman-Brown adjusted Pearson correlation) of a team's average opponent points per 100 possessions (PPP) difference between the second and first halves, after residualizing for opponent offensive rating, home/away, days of rest, and first-half scoring rate (to mitigate regression to the mean). The unit is the team-season.",
    "unit": "team-season",
    "sample": "All 120 team-seasons from NBA regular seasons 2022-23 through 2025-26. Each season is split chronologically into two halves of equal games (first 41 vs. last 41, or as balanced as possible). For each half, per-game H2-H1 opponent PPP differences are computed, adjusted via linear regression on the covariates, and averaged to yield one residualized value per half per team-season.",
    "power": "With 120 team-seasons, the study has 80% power to detect an ICC of approximately 0.35 at alpha=0.05 (one-sided test). The power to detect an ICC of 0.20 (similar to prior player-level metrics) is below 50%. Therefore, the test is underpowered for small but potentially meaningful effect sizes.",
    "controls": "Per-game covariates: opponent season offensive rating (points per 100 possessions), game location (home/away), days of rest for both teams, first-half points per 100 possessions for the opponent (to control for regression to the mean). The H2-H1 difference is first regressed on these covariates game by game, and the residuals are averaged per half.",
    "falsification": "Null hypothesis: the between-team variance component (ICC) is zero. Assessed via a permutation test that randomly shuffles team identities within each season-half while preserving the half structure, computing the ICC on each shuffle, and comparing the observed ICC to the null distribution. Also, a negative control: the same reliability test applied to a metric known to be unreliable, such as random halves of opponent second-half scoring only, should produce a non-significant ICC.",
    "multiplicity": "A single primary endpoint (residualized opponent H2-H1 PPP difference) and a single reliability metric (Spearman-Brown adjusted split-half correlation) are specified, so no multiplicity adjustment is needed.",
    "decision_rule": "If the observed Spearman-Brown adjusted correlation is significantly greater than zero at a 5% level (one-sided) and the 90% confidence interval lower bound exceeds 0.10, the hypothesis is considered supported. Otherwise, if the confidence interval includes zero, the hypothesis is rejected. If the study is underpowered (achieved power <80% for the observed effect), the result is classified as uncertain and the hypothesis not rejected.",
    "data_version": "NBA per-game play-by-play and matchup data for seasons 2022-23 through 2025-26, with derived possession-level points per 100 possessions. The analysis uses exactly the data version available on 2026-10-06 as described in the data catalog."
